// A value-returning free function colliding with a class name, in both
// declaration orders.
//
// Registering a routine reused whatever already sat under its (lowered) name,
// following aliases. A class publishes its constructor under the bare class
// name, so `int widget(...)` adopted `widget.widget`'s symbol and overwrote its
// type, type_def and arity: the VOID constructor was recorded as returning Int.
// The VM decides whether a RETURN leaves a value from that recorded type, so
// `new Widget(5)` popped a bogus "return value" -- the constructor's own `v`
// parameter -- and assigned it to w, failing with
// "Type mismatch. Cannot assign INTEGER to POINTER for symbol 'w'".
//
// It only reproduced when the class came first (the alias has to exist before
// the function is registered), so both orders are pinned here: Widget's function
// follows its class, Gadget's precedes it. Each class prints a field set by its
// constructor, which is what the corruption destroyed.
class Widget { int count; Widget(int v) { my.count = v; } }
int widget(Widget t) { return t.count + 100; }

int gadget(int v) { return v + 200; }
class Gadget { int tag; Gadget(int v) { my.tag = v; } }

Widget w = new Widget(5);
writeln(w.count);
writeln(widget(w));

Gadget g = new Gadget(7);
writeln(g.tag);
writeln(gadget(3));
